<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Tryon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1729">1729</a>–<a href="/years/1788">1788</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Tryon was a British army officer and colonial governor of North Carolina from 1765 to 1771 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> from 1771 to 1780. In North Carolina he built Tryon Palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-bern">New Bern</a> and crushed the Regulator movement at Alamance in 1771; in the Revolutionary War he led Loyalist raids in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-tryon.jpg" alt="William Tryon" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was TJ0513 at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tryon1767.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tryon was born on 8 June 1729 at Norbury Park in Surrey, the son of Charles Tryon and Lady Mary Shirley and a grandson of Robert Shirley, 1st Earl Ferrers. He entered the 1st Regiment of Foot Guards as a lieutenant in 1751 and became a lieutenant-colonel in 1758. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> he was wounded in the thigh and head at the Battle of Saint Cast. In 1757 he married Margaret Wake, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> heiress, later the namesake of Wake County, North Carolina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Tryon became governor of North Carolina after the death of Arthur Dobbs in 1765. He worked to expand the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, kept the assembly from meeting so that it could not oppose the Stamp Act, and set up a postal service in 1769. With the architect John Hawks he designed a governor's mansion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-bern">New Bern</a>, which he made the capital; the taxes raised to build it earned it the name Tryon Palace. Those taxes, with fraud by western officials, fed the War of the Regulation, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alamance">Battle of Alamance</a> in May 1771 he had seven alleged Regulators executed.</p><p>He became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on 8 July 1771. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> crisis he gave up trying to land the tea after news of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party">Boston Tea Party</a> arrived, and his mansion burned on 29 December 1773. In October 1775 he took refuge on a British ship in New York Harbor, and in 1776 he and the mayor David Mathews were behind a failed plot to kidnap <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>. In 1777 he raided Danbury and defeated David Wooster and Benedict Arnold at Ridgefield, and in July 1779 his raids burned and plundered most of Fairfield and Norwalk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tryon returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in September 1780, was promoted lieutenant-general in 1782 and died at home on 27 January 1788; he was buried at St Mary's Church, Twickenham. The historian Scott M. Smith credits him with a reputation as a fair, honest and capable administrator, while Henry Laurens, president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a>, wrote in 1779 that his memory ought to be held in everlasting contempt. Fort Tryon Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a> and the town of Tryon, North Carolina, bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Tryon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governor <b>William Tryon</b> built an expensive palace at New Bern, the capital from the 1760s, and crushed the Regulator movement of backcountry farmers protesting corrupt officials at the Battle of Alamance in 1771.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-north-carolina">Province of North Carolina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Norbury Park House, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.27,-0.34&amp;year=1767" aria-label="Norbury Park House on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4542 388 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Tryon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4992 658h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4992 658h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.27,-0.34&amp;year=1767">What was here in 1767? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
